<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marzuban ibn Muhammad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 957</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marzuban ibn Muhammad was a Daylamite ruler of the Sallarid, or Musafirid, dynasty who seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> in 941 after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sajid-dynasty">Sajids</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and extending his authority over Arran, Shirvan and Armenian princes. He faced a Rus raid that captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barda">Barda'a</a> in 943. Captured by the Buyid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rukn-al-dawla">Rukn al-Dawla</a> in 948, he was imprisoned for several years and regained power before his death in 957.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marzuban-ibn-muhammad.jpg" alt="Marzuban ibn Muhammad" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Interfase · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_dirham_of_Marzban_bin_Muhammad_of_Salarids.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marzuban was a son of Muhammad bin Musafir, ruler of Tarum. In 941 he and his brother Wahsudan ibn Muhammad, with their mother's tacit approval, deposed and imprisoned their father, whose harsh rule had alienated many of his subjects, and Wahsudan took Tarum. Abu'l-Qasim Ja'far b. 'Ali, who had fled his post as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> to Daisam, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, then offered Marzuban the chance to overthrow his master. All three were Isma'ilis, which may explain why Ja'far turned to them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Daisam's Dailamite troops went over to Marzuban, who occupied Ardebil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>. Ja'far, fearing for his position, invited Daisam back to Tabriz and killed the Dailamite leaders there, and Daisam won over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, but Marzuban defeated them and besieged Daisam in Ardebil until he surrendered in 942 or 943. The town paid a heavy tribute and had to pull down its wall. Marzuban then pushed north and west: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> became his tributary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, and by 945 he held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvin">Dvin</a>.</p><p>When the Rus came up the Kura River and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barda">Barda</a>, his larger army was defeated several times, and only after disease had weakened them did he ambush and kill 700. While he besieged Barda, the Hamdanid Nasir al-Daula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> sent his nephew al-Husain b. Sa'id to claim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>; leaving a small force to watch the Rus, Marzuban defeated him in a winter campaign. The Rus then left with their loot and prisoners.</p><p>Around 948, angered by an insult to his envoy, he marched against Ray, held by the Buwayhid Rukn al-Daula, but was defeated at Qazvin and captured. In his absence Daisam returned to power, and the Rawadids and Shaddadids seized lands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvin">Dvin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Marzuban escaped from prison in 953, and his army drove Daisam to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. In 954/5 he made peace with Rukn al-Daula, who married his daughter; he expelled the Shaddadids from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvin">Dvin</a> and made the Rawadids pay tribute. When Daisam took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salmas">Salmas</a> in 955 or 956 with the help of Saif al-Daula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, Marzuban defeated him, and the king of Vaspurakan handed Daisam over to be blinded and imprisoned. Marzuban died in 957. His first will named his sons Justan, Ibrahim and Nasir; a later one named Wahsudan, but he never cancelled the first, and war followed his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marzuban ibn Muhammad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Marzuban ibn Muhammad</b> ruled from Ardabil and Tabriz, extending his authority over Arran, Shirvan and Armenian princes and defending against the Rus raid on Barda'a in 943 to 944.</blockquote><a href="/state/sallarid-dynasty">Sallarid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Sajid Emirate collapsed, <b>Marzuban ibn Muhammad</b> seized Azerbaijan in 941, taking Ardabil and Tabriz and extending his authority to Arran, Shirvan and Armenia.</blockquote><a href="/state/sallarid-emirate">Sallarid Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
